  "id": "COMPOUND:16166 - PROTEIN:5836",
  "verbalization": "Traxillagenin appears to exert its biological effects through a series of mechanistic pathways that ultimately target HUVEC cells. The evidence suggests that Traxillagenin interacts with T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3, which is a key component of the T-cell receptor complex. This interaction is further modulated by Phyllofolactones F and Lup-20(29)-en-3-ol, both of which have been shown to target HUVEC. These findings indicate that Traxillagenin may act as a modulator of T-cell signaling, potentially influencing cellular processes that are relevant to HUVEC function. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Traxillagenin in regulating T-cell activity, which could have implications for cellular processes involving HUVEC. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism involving the modulation of T-cell surface receptors, which may lead to changes in HUVEC behavior. The observed final scores suggest that the relationship between Traxillagenin and HUVEC is supported by a network of interconnected targets, reinforcing the likelihood of a mechanistic link.",
